\title{12 — Baselines on this machine: the numbers RLVM has to beat}
\taxon{Study}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{benchmark}
\tag{arena}
\tag{performance}
\meta{origin}{docs/research/12-baselines.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{Measured 2026-11-24 on the project host (aarch64, Cortex-X925 + Cortex-A725, 20 cores, 121 GB) inside the \code{\verb~|rlvm-arena~} container, with the arena's own checker builds (\code{\verb~|bench/baselines/~}, arena revision \code{\verb~|99195b6b~}) on the arena's own exports (\code{\verb~|work/exports/~}, byte-identical to the published ones). Everything here is a measurement made for this document; nothing is taken from the checkers' READMEs.}

\ul{
\li{Machine-readable results: \code{\verb~|bench/baselines.json~} (every run, medians, ratios, published numbers).}
\li{Raw runs: \code{\verb~|work/baselines/big/runs.jsonl~}, logs in \code{\verb~|work/baselines/big/logs/~}.}
\li{Per-declaration profiles of mathlib: \code{\verb~|work/baselines/mathlib-profile.json~}, \code{\verb~|mathlib-profile.top200.tsv~}, \code{\verb~|mathlib-profile.<profile>.tsv.gz~} (every declaration, most expensive first), \code{\verb~|mathlib-profile.by-name.tsv.gz~} (one row per declaration name with its cost in nano-lean, gruszka, rpylean and sokonanoda).}
\li{Scripts: \src{bench/baselines/big.sh}, \code{\verb~|big-all.sh~}, \code{\verb~|big-summary.py~}, \code{\verb~|profile-mathlib.sh~}, \code{\verb~|instrtap.c~}, \code{\verb~|profile-summary.py~}; commands in section 8 and in \src{bench/README.md}.}
}

\p{The tables between \code{\verb~|<!-- BEGIN ... -->~} and \code{\verb~|<!-- END ... -->~} markers are generated from those files by \code{\verb~|bench/baselines/render-tables.py docs/research/12-baselines.md~}; do not edit them by hand.}

\subtree{
\title{0. Summary}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The number to beat on this machine is 361.3e9 instructions = 60.2 virtual seconds on mathlib} (nano-lean, median of 3 runs, spread 0.04 \%). Then gruszka 418.7e9 (69.8 s, 1.16 x), sokonanoda 504.3e9 (84.1 s, 1.40 x) and rpylean 544.6e9 (90.8 s, 1.51 x): the arena's top four, in the arena's order. After a gap: mathgraph 880.4e9, pestka 1.34e12, nanoda 5.53e12, tenet-jvm 9.31e12 (25.9 virtual minutes with C2; 11.96e12 with the Graal JIT) and the official kernel 11.42e12 (31.7 virtual minutes). All ten accept all three corpora.}
\li{\strong{aarch64 counts are not the published ones.} Native checkers execute 3-10 \% fewer instructions here than on the arena's x86-64 runner (nano-lean 0.915, gruszka 0.906, sokonanoda 0.956, rpylean 0.942, official 0.945); the JVM checker has no stable ratio (1.03 on init, 1.05 on std, 0.951 on mathlib with C2). RLVM's own ratio is unknown, so "ahead of nano-lean on the arena" is only safe below about 355e9 here (59 virtual s); section 3.}
\li{\strong{Instruction counts repeat, times do not.} Spread over 3 runs: at most 0.3 \% for the fast checkers on mathlib, 0.7 \% on init and std; up to 5 \% for tenet-jvm on init. Wall time of the same command varied by up to a factor of 2 with the load of the host (gruszka on mathlib: 18.1 s to 35.3 s).}
\li{\strong{Worker threads are free in the metric.} 1, 2, 4 and 8 workers cost nano-lean 361.0e9, 361.2e9, 361.3e9 and 361.9e9 instructions; gruszka +1.0 \% and sokonanoda +3.5 \% from 1 to 4 workers; section 4.}
\li{\strong{Reading the export is 7-11 \% of a leader's run}: 37-41e9 instructions for 6.3 GB, about 6 per byte; section 5.2.}
\li{\strong{Checking has no hot spot.} Measured per declaration with instruction counters: the most expensive declaration of mathlib is 0.35 \% of nano-lean's checking cost, the top 200 are 5.6 \%, the top 1,000 are 12 \%, the top 10,000 are 36 \%; half the cost is in 23,301 declarations and the other half in 715,866. gruszka, sokonanoda and rpylean show the same curve. Theorems are 89 \% of the cost, definitions 10 \%, inductive types under 1 \%; the compiler-generated \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} theorems alone are 18.5 \%; section 5.}
\li{\strong{A checker that wants to be first must average under 434,000 instructions per constant} (median 78,000) after reading 112 million lines at about 365 instructions each. Per-declaration fixed costs and run-time compilation have to fit into that; section 6.}
\li{\strong{Instruction count is not time.} The JVM baseline with the Graal JIT instead of C2 takes 1.4 \% more CPU time on mathlib and 28.4 \% more instructions (7.0 \% more on init). Choices for RLVM have to be compared by instruction count, on mathlib.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{1. What was measured, and how}

\p{\strong{Checkers.} The ten baselines built by \src{bench/baselines/build-checker.sh} from the arena's yaml recipes (\src{bench/README.md}, "Baseline checkers"), run through the generated wrappers \code{\verb~|bench/baselines/NAME.sh~}, i.e. the yaml's \code{\verb~|run:~} line through \code{\verb~|sh -c~} with cwd = the build tree, \code{\verb~|$IN~} = the export, \code{\verb~|LC_ALL=C~}:}

\table{
\tr{\th{baseline}\th{version}\th{run line: workers}\th{note}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{v0.4.2 \code{\verb~|5cd4a25~}}\td{\code{\verb~|nl-fast -q -j 4~}: 4}\td{arena \verb~|#~1 on mathlib}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{\code{\verb~|ebc80dc~}}\td{\code{\verb~|scripts/arena-run "$IN" 4~}: \code{\verb~|susz pack~}, then 4 workers}\td{arena \verb~|#~2}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{\code{\verb~|9a021b6~}}\td{\code{\verb~|--threads 4~}: 4}\td{arena \verb~|#~3}}
\tr{\td{rpylean}\td{v2026.11.11 \code{\verb~|bbf354d~}}\td{\code{\verb~|./rpylean-c check~}: 1}\td{arena \verb~|#~4; RPython, meta-tracing JIT}}
\tr{\td{mathgraph}\td{\code{\verb~|flash~} \code{\verb~|78c7502~}}\td{\code{\verb~|num_threads~} 2}\td{accepts 8 bad proofs on the small tests, so not a ranking competitor}}
\tr{\td{pestka}\td{\code{\verb~|cea66e6~}}\td{\code{\verb~|bin/arena-run "$IN" 4~}}\td{}}
\tr{\td{nanoda}\td{\code{\verb~|0da9f98~}}\td{\code{\verb~|num_threads~} 4}\td{}}
\tr{\td{tenet-jvm-c2}\td{0.1.5 \code{\verb~|4cc6e20~}}\td{\code{\verb~|bin/tenet-jvm~}: 1 + GC and JIT threads}\td{the project's GraalVM CE 25 with \code{\verb~|-XX:-UseJVMCICompiler~} (C2): the closest to the arena's stock OpenJDK}}
\tr{\td{tenet-jvm}\td{0.1.5 \code{\verb~|4cc6e20~}}\td{the same}\td{the same JVM with its default Graal JIT; not what the arena runs, but what a GraalVM-hosted checker gets}}
\tr{\td{official}\td{Lean v4.35.0}\td{\code{\verb~|leanchecker --from-export~}: 1}\td{the C++ kernel}}
}

\p{\strong{Corpora.} \code{\verb~|work/exports/init.ndjson~} (348 MB, 59,979 constants), \code{\verb~|std.ndjson~} (584 MB, 103,885 constants), \code{\verb~|mathlib.ndjson~} (6,326,141,213 bytes, 112,081,718 lines, 107,037,712 expressions, 739,167 constants; sha256 \code{\verb~|f749dfd084fc…~}, equal to the arena's).}

\p{\strong{One run} = one fresh container, nothing else in it:}

\pre{\verb~|
bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network bench/measure.sh --timeout T \
    --in work/exports/CORPUS.ndjson -o RAW.json -c 'bench/baselines/NAME.sh "$IN"'
~}

\ul{
\li{\code{\verb~|--arena-limits~}: 8 performance cores (CPUs 5-9,15-17) and 16 GB, the size of the arena runner; \code{\verb~|--no-network~}.}
\li{\src{bench/measure.sh} is the arena's measurement (\code{\verb~|lka.py~}, \code{\verb~|measure_perf_with_fallback~}): \code{\verb~|perf stat~} over the whole process tree, all threads and children, user-space instructions; \code{\verb~|virtual time = instructions / 6e9~}, the arena's ranking metric; CPU time = perf's task-clock; wall time = perf's duration_time; max RSS from GNU time.}
\li{Timeout \code{\verb~|T~} = 2 x the arena's (init 370 s, std 600 s, mathlib 4120 s); no run came near it.}
\li{Runs of the series were made one after the other (\src{bench/baselines/big-all.sh}): 3 runs of every baseline on init and std; on mathlib 3 runs of the six fast checkers and 1 run of the four slow ones. The single run of the official kernel on mathlib (single-threaded, deterministic to 0.05 \%) ran next to the series on two performance cores of its own (CPUs 18,19, 16 GB), to save half an hour.}
\li{The figure quoted for a (checker, corpus) is the \strong{median} over its runs; \code{\verb~|spread~} = (max - min) / median.}
}

\p{\strong{Caveat on times.} Other agents were using the host during the whole series (1-minute load average between 5 and 35 on 20 cores, at times on the same 8 cores). Instruction counts do not depend on that (the three mathlib runs of nano-lean differ by 0.04 \% while their wall time went from 25.5 s to 42.1 s); wall and CPU times do. The wall column therefore gives the minimum and the median; read it as "no better than".}
}

