% <!-- END big:corpora -->

\p{Reading the tables:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{The target.} nano-lean checks mathlib in 361.3e9 instructions = 60.2 virtual seconds here (65.8 s published). gruszka needs 1.16 x that, sokonanoda 1.40 x, rpylean 1.51 x. These four are the arena's top four, in the same order, with nearly the same gaps (section 3).}
\li{\strong{Init and std are not mathlib in small.} gruszka and nano-lean are within 1.5 \% of each other on init and std (gruszka ahead on std) but 16 \% apart on mathlib; sokonanoda is 1.25-1.27 x the fastest on the small corpora and 1.40 x on mathlib, rpylean 1.36-1.40 x and 1.51 x. Decide on mathlib (or its slices), not on Init.}
\li{\strong{Scale.} mathlib costs nano-lean 16.2 times its Init count, for 18.2 times the bytes and 12.3 times the constants.}
\li{\strong{The JVM baseline is far away.} tenet-jvm with C2 costs 10.4 x the fastest on init, 10.4 x on std and 25.8 x on mathlib (9.31e12, 25.9 virtual minutes). Its max RSS is 4.4-4.9 GiB on init and std and 11.9 GiB on mathlib, where the Rust leaders need 2.5-5.1 GiB.}
\li{\strong{Graal JIT against C2}, same bytecode, same JVM, default settings of \code{\verb~|bin/tenet-jvm~}: +7.0 \% instructions on init (247.0e9 against 230.9e9), +3.8 \% on std and +28.4 \% on mathlib (11.96e12 against 9.31e12; one run each there, a JVM run varies by 1-2 \%). On mathlib the two runs took about the same CPU time (1,160 s and 1,144 s) and wall time (1,083 s and 1,064 s): the Graal run retired 28 \% more instructions in 1.4 \% more CPU time (10.3 against 8.1 instructions per nanosecond). The arena ranks by instructions, so by its metric the Graal JIT is the worse choice for this checker although the clock sees no difference. The cause was not investigated.}
\li{\strong{The official kernel} needs 11.42e12 (31.7 virtual minutes, 1757 s of wall time on one X925 core, 9.9 GiB): 31.6 x nano-lean. nanoda, the reference Rust port of the same algorithm, 5.53e12 (15.3 x).}
\li{\strong{Wall time} of the least-disturbed mathlib run with 4 workers on 8 cores: gruszka 18 s, nano-lean 26 s, sokonanoda 30 s; rpylean (one thread) 118 s.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{3. aarch64 against the published x86-64 numbers}

\p{The arena runs on x86-64, so its published counts are not ours. The ratio ours/published per checker, on the 230 small tests (totals of \src{bench/baselines/validate.sh}, previous infra step) and on the three big corpora:}

% <!-- BEGIN big:arch -->

\subtree{
\title{aarch64 (this host) / x86-64 (arena) instruction counts}

\table{
\tr{\th{checker}\th{230 small tests}\th{init}\th{std}\th{mathlib}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{0.934}\td{0.915}\td{0.918}\td{0.915}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{0.926}\td{0.909}\td{0.905}\td{0.906}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{0.933}\td{0.962}\td{0.959}\td{0.956}}
\tr{\td{rpylean}\td{1.006}\td{0.927}\td{0.929}\td{0.942}}
\tr{\td{mathgraph}\td{0.991}\td{0.967}\td{0.967}\td{0.966}}
\tr{\td{pestka}\td{0.873}\td{0.896}\td{0.899}\td{0.908}}
\tr{\td{nanoda}\td{0.933}\td{0.962}\td{0.962}\td{0.958}}
\tr{\td{tenet-jvm-c2}\td{0.892}\td{1.032}\td{1.049}\td{0.951}}
\tr{\td{tenet-jvm}\td{1.539}\td{1.105}\td{1.089}\td{1.221}}
\tr{\td{official}\td{0.944}\td{0.949}\td{0.950}\td{0.945}}
}
}

% <!-- END big:arch -->

\ul{
\li{\strong{Native checkers: 0.90 to 0.97, stable per checker across the three big corpora} (nano-lean 0.915-0.918, gruszka 0.905-0.909, sokonanoda 0.956-0.962, nanoda 0.958-0.962, official 0.944-0.950, mathgraph 0.966-0.967, pestka 0.896-0.908, rpylean 0.927-0.942). The same program executes 3-10 \% fewer instructions on aarch64; compilers, PGO profiles and \code{\verb~|-C target-cpu=native~} differ too (\src{bench/README.md}, "Differences from the arena's build").}
\li{\strong{The ranking carries over.} gruszka / nano-lean on mathlib is 1.159 here and 1.170 published; sokonanoda / nano-lean 1.396 and 1.336; rpylean / nano-lean 1.507 and 1.464.}
\li{\strong{The JVM checker has no stable ratio}: tenet-jvm with C2 is 1.03 on init, 1.05 on std and 0.951 on mathlib. The comparison is not clean either: the arena's JVM is the stock OpenJDK of its nix flake, ours is GraalVM CE 25 with its Graal compiler switched off, and what GC and JIT threads execute depends on timing and on the heap the JVM chooses. It is the only JVM data point there is, and it does not support assuming the native checkers' 0.92 for RLVM. (The 0.892 and 1.539 in the small-tests column are one test, \code{\verb~|bugs/nat-shift-huge~}, where tenet-jvm runs out of memory; without it 0.96 and 1.09.)}
\li{\strong{Consequence for RLVM's target.} nano-lean's published count is 394.9e9. If RLVM's ratio (count here / count on the arena) is anywhere between the lowest and the highest seen above on a big corpus for a baseline in the arena's configuration (0.90 to 1.05), nano-lean's arena count corresponds to between 355e9 and 415e9 here. Until RLVM has been measured on an x86-64 machine with these same scripts, read \strong{355e9 here (59 virtual s) as "ahead of nano-lean whatever the ratio"} and 361e9 as "ahead on this machine".}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{4. Thread scaling}

\p{nano-lean, gruszka and sokonanoda run with 4 workers on the arena. The same three on mathlib with 1, 2 and 8 workers (one run each; the 4-worker row is the median of the three arena-configuration runs):}

