\table{
\tr{\th{checker}\th{threads}\th{instructions}\th{virtual time}\th{vs 1 thread}\th{CPU s}\th{wall s}\th{max RSS MiB}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{1}\td{361.0e9}\td{60.16 s}\td{1.000}\td{90.5}\td{89.9}\td{5,157}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{2}\td{361.2e9}\td{60.20 s}\td{1.001}\td{82.5}\td{43.7}\td{5,157}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{4}\td{361.3e9}\td{60.22 s}\td{1.001}\td{107.2}\td{36.9}\td{5,157}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{8}\td{361.9e9}\td{60.31 s}\td{1.002}\td{99.1}\td{17.9}\td{5,452}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{1}\td{414.6e9}\td{69.10 s}\td{1.000}\td{61.3}\td{58.1}\td{2,211}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{2}\td{417.0e9}\td{69.50 s}\td{1.006}\td{71.7}\td{36.8}\td{2,301}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{4}\td{418.7e9}\td{69.78 s}\td{1.010}\td{75.9}\td{22.3}\td{2,592}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{8}\td{421.6e9}\td{70.26 s}\td{1.017}\td{68.2}\td{14.4}\td{2,831}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{1}\td{487.2e9}\td{81.19 s}\td{1.000}\td{95.2}\td{94.4}\td{4,630}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{2}\td{497.2e9}\td{82.86 s}\td{1.021}\td{90.3}\td{48.1}\td{4,759}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{4}\td{504.3e9}\td{84.05 s}\td{1.035}\td{113.2}\td{35.7}\td{5,228}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{8}\td{510.7e9}\td{85.12 s}\td{1.048}\td{106.6}\td{19.7}\td{5,607}}
}
}

% <!-- END big:threads -->

\ul{
\li{\strong{More workers cost almost nothing in the arena's metric.} From 1 to 4 workers nano-lean's count rises by 0.09 \% (361.0e9 to 361.3e9), gruszka's by 1.0 \%, sokonanoda's by 3.5 \%; from 1 to 8 workers by 0.24 \%, 1.7 \% and 4.8 \%. The instruction count of these checkers is, to within a few percent, their single-threaded work.}
\li{\strong{What the workers buy is wall time}, which the arena shows but does not rank by: nano-lean 90 s with one worker, 18 s with eight; gruszka 58 s and 14 s; sokonanoda 94 s and 20 s (a busy host; see section 1).}
\li{Memory grows little: nano-lean 5.0 GiB with 1 to 4 workers and 5.3 GiB with 8; gruszka 2.2 to 2.8 GiB; sokonanoda 4.5 to 5.5 GiB.}
\li{The three leaders declare 4 threads on an 8-vCPU runner. With 8 workers they finished mathlib here in 14-20 s instead of 18-30 s (least-disturbed runs), for 0.2-1.3 \% more instructions than with 4.}
\li{For RLVM: checking declarations in parallel is free in the ranking metric \strong{if} idle workers sleep instead of spinning and workers do not repeat each other's work. Section 5.6 shows that this holds per declaration, not only in total. What is not free for a JVM is the runtime's own parallelism: GC and JIT compiler threads are sized by the CPUs the JVM sees, and every instruction they execute is counted.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5. Where the instructions go on mathlib}

\subtree{
\title{5.1 How the profiles were obtained}

\p{Four of the baselines can print something per declaration. None of them can count instructions per declaration on Linux, so the counts were added from outside, without changing the checkers:}

\table{
\tr{\th{checker}\th{switch (found in its source)}\th{what it prints}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{\code{\verb~|nl-fast --trace~} (\code{\verb~|src/fast.rs~})}\td{\code{\verb~|start IDX NAME~} and \code{\verb~|end IDX elapsed T arena N bytes~} on stderr around every declaration}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{\code{\verb~|nl-fast --import-only~}}\td{stops after reading the export}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{\code{\verb~|SK_DECL_INSTR=1~} (\code{\verb~|src/main.rs~})}\td{\code{\verb~|DI<TAB>index<TAB>name<TAB>instructions<TAB>ns<TAB>ticks …~} after every declaration; the instruction field is 0 on Linux (it is read from a macOS-only API)}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{\code{\verb~|--verbose~}, \code{\verb~|--parse-only~} (\code{\verb~|src/main.rs~}, \code{\verb~|src/report.rs~})}\td{one line per declaration with kind and wall time in ms (buffered on stdout)}}
\tr{\td{rpylean}\td{\code{\verb~|check --print jsonl~} (\code{\verb~|rpylean/cli.py~})}\td{one JSON object per declaration: \code{\verb~|elapsed~}, \code{\verb~|gc_elapsed~}, \code{\verb~|bytes_allocated~}, \code{\verb~|live_memory~}, \code{\verb~|peak_growth~}}}
\tr{\td{official, nanoda, pestka, mathgraph}\td{none for exports}\td{—}}
\tr{\td{tenet-jvm}\td{\code{\verb~|TENET_TRACE=1~} prints the name before each item, no cost}\td{not used}}
}

\p{\code{\verb~|bench/baselines/instrtap.c~} is a small \code{\verb~|LD_PRELOAD~} library that interposes \code{\verb~|write~} and \code{\verb~|fwrite~}: whenever the checker starts one of those lines, it first prints \code{\verb~|#I <tid> <count>~}, the calling thread's user-space instruction count from its own \code{\verb~|perf_event_open~} counter (INST_RETIRED, user mode, one event per PMU of this big.LITTLE host, summed). That is the counter \code{\verb~|perf stat -e instructions:u~} uses, per thread. The difference of two such lines of a thread is the cost of what the thread did in between. Self-test (\code{\verb~|profile-mathlib.sh selftest~}): a loop of 1,200,000,000 instructions between a \code{\verb~|start~} and an \code{\verb~|end~} line reads as 1,200,000,522 on each of 4 threads.}

\p{What an interval contains differs per checker, which matters for the small declarations:}

\ul{
\li{\strong{nano-lean}: \code{\verb~|start~} to \code{\verb~|end~} of the same declaration: the check and nothing else (a few hundred instructions of printing). This is the cleanest profile and the reference below.}
\li{\strong{gruszka}: from one \code{\verb~|DI~} line to the next of the same worker: the check plus printing the previous \code{\verb~|DI~} line, which is long. The same run without \code{\verb~|SK_DECL_INSTR~} and without the library is 22.8e9 instructions cheaper, i.e. about 31,600 per declaration; that constant is subtracted from every declaration (raw values are kept in the \code{\verb~|.tsv.gz~}). A "declaration" of gruszka is an export record: an inductive block counts once (721,845 records against 739,167 constants).}
\li{\strong{rpylean} reads and checks declaration by declaration, so an interval is "read this declaration's lines, check it", plus whatever the runtime does in between: collections, table growth, JIT compilation. A second view uses rpylean's own \code{\verb~|elapsed~} (wall time of the check alone).}
\li{\strong{sokonanoda}: wall time only (its lines are buffered, so the library cannot attribute them). Inductive types, constructors and recursors are reported with 0 ms.}
}

\p{All profile runs used one worker (4-worker runs in 5.6) and were measured as a whole with \src{bench/measure.sh} as well; the instrumented single-worker runs cost 1.1 \% more than the plain single-worker runs for nano-lean (364.9e9 against 361.0e9), 0.9 \% for sokonanoda (491.4e9 against 487.2e9), 1.9 \% for rpylean (554.8e9 against 544.6e9) and 5.9 \% for gruszka (407.8e9 against 385.0e9 for \code{\verb~|gruszka export.susz 1~} alone).}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.2 Reading the export against checking}

% <!-- BEGIN profile:split -->

\subtree{
\title{Instructions spent reading mathlib.ndjson, and what is left for checking}

\table{
\tr{\th{checker}\th{arena run (4 workers)}\th{reading the export}\th{share}\th{checking}\th{how measured}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean}\td{361.32e9}\td{40.88e9}\td{11.3 \%}\td{320.44e9}\td{\code{\verb~|nl-fast -q -j 4 --import-only~}}}
\tr{\td{gruszka}\td{418.66e9}\td{37.49e9 = pack 29.60e9 + load 7.90e9}\td{9.0 \%}\td{381.16e9}\td{\code{\verb~|susz pack --no-index~} measured alone; load = the 1-worker run outside declarations}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda}\td{504.31e9}\td{36.91e9}\td{7.3 \%}\td{467.40e9}\td{\code{\verb~|sokonanoda --parse-only --threads 4~}}}
\tr{\td{rpylean}\td{544.61e9}\td{interleaved}\td{19.0 \% of wall time}\td{-}\td{wall 124 s, of which checking 100 s (GC inside checks 3.1 s)}}
}
}

% <!-- END profile:split -->

\ul{
\li{\strong{Reading mathlib costs the three Rust leaders 37-41e9 instructions}: 7-11 \% of their totals, 5.8-6.5 instructions per byte of NDJSON, 330-365 per line (6,326,141,213 bytes, 112,081,718 lines). gruszka's packer alone (\code{\verb~|susz pack~}, NDJSON to its binary Susz format) is 29.6e9 = 4.7 per byte and 264 per line; loading the packed file into the environment is another 7.9e9. The parts add up: pack + \code{\verb~|gruszka export.susz 4~} = 29.60e9 + 389.11e9 = 418.71e9 against 418.66e9 for the arena run line.}
\li{At nano-lean's level, reading is one ninth of the whole budget. A reader that costs 20 instructions per byte would spend 126e9 instructions on mathlib, a third of nano-lean's whole run.}
\li{nano-lean reads the whole file before it checks anything (5.2 s on one thread, 1.2 GB/s) and reaches its maximum RSS there: \code{\verb~|--import-only~} already peaks at 5,157 MiB, the same as the full run.}
\li{rpylean interleaves reading and checking; 19 \% of its wall time lies outside the per-declaration checks (reading, collections between declarations, table growth); inside the checks rpylean itself reports 3.1 s of collection time.}
}
}

