% <!-- END profile:classes -->

\ul{
\li{\strong{There is no hot spot.} The most expensive declaration of mathlib is 0.35 \% of nano-lean's checking cost (1.12e9 instructions, 0.19 virtual seconds). The top 10 are 1.25 \%, the top 200 are 5.6 \%, the top 1,000 are 12.2 \%, the top 10,000 are 36 \%. Half of the cost is in the 23,301 most expensive declarations (3.2 \% of them), 90 \% in 212,183 (29 \%).}
\li{\strong{All four checkers see the same shape}, whatever their design: the top 200 are 5.7 \% for gruszka, 6.7 \% for sokonanoda (wall time) and 4.6 \% for rpylean (its own check time).}
\li{\strong{The bulk is in mid-size declarations.} For nano-lean, 58,362 declarations of 1e6 to 1e7 instructions carry 46.5 \% of the checking cost, 266,630 of 1e5 to 1e6 carry 27.0 \%, and 3,273 of 1e7 to 1e8 carry 19.1 \%. The 410,859 declarations below 1e5 instructions (56 \% of all) carry 4.6 \%; the 43 above 1e8 carry 2.7 \%.}
\li{Mean cost per constant 435,000 instructions, median 78,000 (nano-lean); 522,000 and 114,000 for gruszka's 721,845 records.}
\li{mathlib is therefore 739,167 small, independent jobs, and \strong{per-declaration fixed cost is a first-order term}: 10,000 instructions of set-up or tear-down per declaration are 7.4e9, 2 \% of nano-lean's total; the median declaration costs nano-lean only eight times that.}
}
}

