\ul{
\li{\strong{Theorems are 89 \% of the checking cost} in all three profiles, definitions 10 \%, everything else together under 1 \%. Inductive types with their constructors and recursors are 0.7 \% for nano-lean and 0.9 \% for gruszka (sokonanoda does not time them): the speed of the inductive-type machinery does not show in the ranking, only its correctness does.}
\li{An average theorem costs nano-lean 0.54e6 instructions, an average definition 0.18e6.}
\li{\strong{Compiler-generated proofs are a large share}: the 100,876 \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} constants (proof terms that Lean abstracts out of definitions and tactic blocks) are 18.5 \% of the cost. \code{\verb~|noConfusion~} is 1.2 \%; the other generated families (\code{\verb~|match_N~}, \code{\verb~|eq_N~}, \code{\verb~|injEq~}, \code{\verb~|sizeOf_spec~}, \code{\verb~|casesOn~}, \code{\verb~|congr_simp~}, ...) are below 1 \% each, about 5 \% together.}
\li{By namespace: \code{\verb~|_private~} (mostly those \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} and private lemmas) 18.5 \%; \code{\verb~|CategoryTheory~} 13.0 \% with 10.7 \% of the declarations; \code{\verb~|Std~} 3.7 \%; \code{\verb~|AlgebraicGeometry~} 2.7 \% with 1.1 \% of the declarations. Lean's own \code{\verb~|Lean.*~} (54,168 declarations) is 1.2 \%.}
}
}

