\subtree{
\title{Agreement between checkers: declarations in common among the N most expensive}

\table{
\tr{\th{pair}\th{top 10}\th{top 50}\th{top 200}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean.j1 / gruszka.w1}\td{7}\td{38}\td{153}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean.j1 / sokonanoda.t1}\td{5}\td{32}\td{130}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean.j1 / rpylean (check wall time)}\td{5}\td{28}\td{85}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean.j1 / rpylean}\td{1}\td{13}\td{95}}
\tr{\td{gruszka.w1 / sokonanoda.t1}\td{6}\td{33}\td{131}}
\tr{\td{gruszka.w1 / rpylean (check wall time)}\td{5}\td{26}\td{84}}
\tr{\td{gruszka.w1 / rpylean}\td{1}\td{12}\td{90}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda.t1 / rpylean (check wall time)}\td{6}\td{28}\td{102}}
\tr{\td{sokonanoda.t1 / rpylean}\td{1}\td{14}\td{98}}
\tr{\td{rpylean (check wall time) / rpylean}\td{3}\td{18}\td{128}}
}

\p{gruszka / nano-lean, same declarations, 1 worker each: 1.173 in total; per declaration (the 61,678 above 1e6 instructions in nano-lean): p05 0.414, p25 0.898, median 1.164, p75 1.373, p95 1.739.}
}

% <!-- END profile:agreement -->

\ul{
\li{\strong{The checkers agree on what is expensive, less on the order.} nano-lean and gruszka share 153 of their 200 most expensive declarations and 7 of their top 10; nano-lean and sokonanoda 130 and 5. The most expensive declaration is the same in all four: \code{\verb~|…sym2x_add_sub_eq_addSubMap_sym2x._proof_1_8~} (1.12e9 instructions for nano-lean, 1.89e9 for gruszka, 860 ms for sokonanoda, 887 ms for rpylean).}
\li{\strong{Neither leader dominates the other declaration by declaration.} Over the 61,678 declarations that cost nano-lean more than 1e6 instructions, gruszka's cost is 1.16 x nano-lean's at the median, but 0.41 x at the 5th percentile and 1.74 x at the 95th. Each is fast where the other is not, so neither is at a floor.}
\li{Going by their names (not checked against the terms), the heavy declarations are of a few families: auxiliary proofs of polynomial identities on elliptic curves and division sequences (\code{\verb~|…EllipticCurve…._proof_1_N~}, \code{\verb~|IsEllipticNet.atomRel…~}), bit-blasting lemmas of \code{\verb~|Std.Tactic.BVDecide~} (\code{\verb~|…goCache…._mutual~}), string and array lemmas of core (\code{\verb~|fromUTF8Lossy…~}, \code{\verb~|extract_append~}), and coherence proofs in category theory and algebraic geometry.}
\li{\strong{rpylean's instruction intervals need care.} 16 of its 25 largest intervals belong to declarations whose own check took under 4 ms; the interval then holds 0.4e9 to 0.96e9 instructions of runtime work that happened to fall between two declarations (my reading: major collections, whose cost grows with the heap; not verified). That is real cost (the 100 largest intervals hold 7.0 \% of rpylean's per-declaration total, the 100 longest checks 3.5 \% of its check time), but it is not the declaration's. Rank rpylean by \code{\verb~|by_check_wall_time~} in the JSON.}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{5.6 One worker against four, per declaration}

% <!-- BEGIN profile:workers -->

\subtree{
\title{Per-declaration totals with 1 and 4 workers (instrumented runs)}

\table{
\tr{\th{profile}\th{workers}\th{whole run}\th{inside declarations}\th{outside}\th{top 200 share}\th{median}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean.j1}\td{1}\td{364.88e9}\td{321.55e9}\td{43.34e9}\td{5.6 \%}\td{77,674}}
\tr{\td{nano-lean.j4}\td{4}\td{365.40e9}\td{322.07e9}\td{43.33e9}\td{5.6 \%}\td{77,985}}
\tr{\td{gruszka.w1}\td{1}\td{407.84e9}\td{399.95e9}\td{7.90e9}\td{5.7 \%}\td{0.11e6}}
