}
}

% <!-- END profile:workers -->

\ul{
\li{nano-lean: the sum over all declarations is 321.55e9 with one worker and 322.07e9 with four (+0.16 \%); what lies outside declarations (import, reordering, start-up) is 43.3e9 in both. Four workers, each with caches of its own, do not repeat a measurable amount of work, and the median declaration costs the same (77,674 and 77,985 instructions).}
\li{gruszka: 399.95e9 and 404.16e9 (+1.1 \%; raw intervals, printing included), 7.9e9 outside in both.}
\li{sokonanoda has no per-declaration counts; its summed per-declaration wall time is 80.9 s with one worker and 98.0 s with four on a busier host, which is not a clean comparison. Its instruction total rises by 3.5 \%.}
}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{6. What this means for RLVM}

\ol{
\li{\strong{The ladder} (mathlib, this machine, instructions): 361.3e9 nano-lean, 418.7e9 gruszka, 504.3e9 sokonanoda, 544.6e9 rpylean; then 880.4e9, 1.34e12, 5.53e12, 9.31e12 (the only JVM checker) and 11.42e12 (official). Below 544.6e9 is top four here; below about 355e9 is first whatever RLVM's aarch64/x86-64 ratio is (section 3).}
\li{\p{\strong{The budget of a 60-second checker}, as nano-lean spends it:}
\table{
\tr{\th{part}\th{instructions}\th{per unit}}
\tr{\td{reading the 6.3 GB export}\td{40.9e9 (11 \%)}\td{6.5 per byte, 365 per line, 382 per exported expression}}
\tr{\td{checking 739,167 constants}\td{320.4e9 (89 \%)}\td{434,000 per constant on average, 78,000 at the median; 2,990 per exported expression}}
\tr{\td{start-up, reordering, teardown}\td{under 2.5e9 (0.7 \%)}\td{upper bound: the 1-worker trace run outside declarations minus the import-only run, tracing included}}
}
\p{gruszka reads for 37.5e9 (pack 29.6e9 + load 7.9e9) and checks for 381e9; sokonanoda 36.9e9 and 467e9.}}
\li{\p{\strong{No warm-up to hide behind.} The cost is spread over hundreds of thousands of declarations (5.3), and each declaration's own terms are used once. What is hot for the whole run is the kernel's generic machinery (weak-head normalisation, definitional equality, inference, substitution or evaluation, hash-consing), not anything derived from one declaration. A run-time compiler pays for itself only on that machinery, and what it spends is counted: the instructions of JIT compiler threads are part of the total.}
\p{\strong{Instructions and time are different currencies.} The data point here is tenet-jvm, the same bytecode on the same JVM: with the Graal JIT instead of C2 it executes +7.0 \% instructions on init, +3.8 \% on std and +28.4 \% on mathlib, in 1.4 \% more CPU time on mathlib. A compiler, a collector or a coding style that is equally fast on the clock can cost a quarter more in the ranking, and Init does not predict it. Compare RLVM's alternatives (native image or JVM, C2 or Graal, GC and heap settings, thread counts) by instruction count on mathlib itself.}}
\li{\strong{Per-declaration fixed cost is a first-order term.} 739,167 declarations: every 10,000 instructions of per-declaration set-up, cache reset, scheduling or bookkeeping are 7.4e9 = 2 \% of the leader's total, and the median declaration only costs 78,000. gruszka's \code{\verb~|DI~} line, a single formatted line per declaration, cost it 31,600 instructions each, 5.9 \% of its run.}
\li{\strong{Theorems are the workload} (89 \%), a fifth of it compiler-generated \code{\verb~|_proof_N~} terms; definitions 10 \%; inductive types, constructors and recursors under 1 \%. Everything that is neither a theorem nor a definition can be written for clarity and correctness with little regard to speed.}
\li{\strong{Parallel checking is free, runtime threads are not.} nano-lean pays 0.09 \% more instructions for 4 workers than for 1 and per declaration nothing (5.6). For a JVM the comparable risk is elsewhere (not measured here): parallel GC and JIT threads sized by the 8 CPUs it sees. Measure with \code{\verb~|--arena-limits~} only.}
\li{\strong{The leaders are not at a floor.} Declaration by declaration gruszka is between 0.41 x and 1.74 x nano-lean (5th to 95th percentile over the declarations above 1e6 instructions), and it is the cheaper of the two on 201,657 of the 721,844 declarations both name (28 \%). Taking the cheaper of the two on every declaration gives 289.8e9 instructions of checking, against 321.5e9 for nano-lean and 377.1e9 for gruszka alone: 10 \% below the leader with techniques that already exist in one of the two (computed from \code{\verb~|mathlib-profile.by-name.tsv.gz~}; gruszka's figures carry the uniform correction of 5.1).}
\li{\strong{Memory.} Peak RSS on mathlib: gruszka 2.5 GiB, pestka 3.5, rpylean 4.4, nano-lean 5.0, sokonanoda 5.1, nanoda 5.9, mathgraph 6.4, official 9.9, tenet-jvm 11.9 GiB. The runner has 16 GB; nano-lean reaches its peak while reading (the import alone peaks at 5.0 GiB).}
\li{\strong{What a JVM checker costs today.} tenet-jvm, a Lean kernel in plain Java: 10.4 x the leader on init, 25.8 x on mathlib, 11.9 GiB. That is the distance between "a kernel on a JVM" and the leaderboard's top; it is not explained by the JVM's start-up (0.5-0.9e9 instructions per run on the smallest tests, 0.15-0.25 \% of nano-lean's mathlib count).}
}
}

\subtree{
\title{7. How to measure RLVM the same way}

\p{All from the project root. RLVM's arena run line is the \code{\verb~|run:~} entry of \src{scripts/arena/rlvm.yaml}; \code{\verb~|bench/rlvm/run.sh --print~} prints it (\code{\verb~|--jvm~}, \code{\verb~|--pgo~}, \code{\verb~|--threads N~} select another launcher or worker count). Measure that command, not a wrapper around it.}

\pre{\verb~|
# 0. build: work/dist/bin/rlvm (native image), rlvm-jvm, rlvm-pgo
scripts/build.sh

# 1. one run on mathlib exactly as the arena measures it: one JSON line with
#    exit_code, instructions, virtual_time_s (= instructions / 6e9), cpu_time_s, wall_time_s, max_rss_bytes
bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network bench/measure.sh --timeout 4120 \
    --in work/exports/mathlib.ndjson -c "$(bench/rlvm/run.sh --print)"

# 2. the series the baselines got: 3 runs each on init, std and mathlib, one fresh container per run
#    (records in work/rlvm/big/runs.jsonl, the checker's output in work/rlvm/big/logs/)
bench/baselines/big.sh --runs 3 --out work/rlvm/big --label rlvm --cwd . --cmd "$(bench/rlvm/run.sh --print)"

# 3. RLVM in the same tables as the baselines: instructions, virtual time, ratio to the fastest
bench/baselines/big-summary.py --no-json --runs work/baselines/big/runs.jsonl --runs work/rlvm/big/runs.jsonl

# other launchers and worker counts: give each its own label (a row of its own) ...
bench/baselines/big.sh --runs 3 --out work/rlvm/big --label rlvm-jvm --cwd . --cmd "$(bench/rlvm/run.sh --jvm --print)"
# ... or, for worker counts, a variant threads-N (they appear in the thread-scaling table)
for n in 1 2 8; do
  bench/baselines/big.sh --corpora mathlib --out work/rlvm/big --label rlvm --variant threads-$n --cwd . \
      --cmd "$(bench/rlvm/run.sh --threads $n --print)"
done
# (bench/rlvm/measure.sh --threads 1,4,8 --repeat 3 work/exports/mathlib.ndjson does a similar matrix in one container)

# 4. fast loop while developing: Init (3.7 virtual s for nano-lean) and the mathlib slices
bench/baselines/big.sh --corpora init --out work/rlvm/big --label rlvm --cwd . --cmd "$(bench/rlvm/run.sh --print)"
bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network bench/run-suite.py --perf --tests work/exports/slices \
    --timeout 1200 --label rlvm --cwd . -- "$(bench/rlvm/run.sh --print)"

# 5. a baseline again, for a same-day comparison (instruction counts repeat to well under 1 %)
bench/baselines/big.sh --corpora mathlib --runs 1 --out work/rlvm/big nano-lean gruszka
~}

\p{These command lines were run as written on 2026-11-24 against the RLVM build of that moment (step 1 and the slices on small exports, step 2 on init-prelude and init), to check that they work; no RLVM result is recorded here.}

\p{Rules that keep the numbers comparable with the tables above:}

\ul{
\li{Always \code{\verb~|--arena-limits~} (8 CPUs, 16 GB): a JVM, and a native image's serial or parallel GC, size their threads and heap by what they see, and their instruction count follows.}
\li{Always the instruction count, never wall or CPU time, and always the whole command: start-up, reading the export, JIT compiler threads and GC threads are all counted by the arena.}
\li{A JVM's count varies by 1-2 \% per run on a workload of this size (5 \% was seen on init for tenet-jvm), the native baselines' by less than 1 \%: take the median of at least 3 runs before claiming a difference.}
\li{The exit status must be 0: the arena takes the mathlib figure only from an accepted run, and a checker without one sorts after all that have one (\code{\verb~|lka.py~}, "Track mathlib performance metrics only if the test was accepted").}
\li{Compare with the numbers in this document (aarch64), not with the arena's web page (x86-64); section 3 says how far apart they are.}
}

\p{\strong{Per-declaration profile of RLVM.} If RLVM prints a line at the start and at the end of every declaration on stderr (as \code{\verb~|nl-fast --trace~} does: \code{\verb~|start <index> <name>~} / \code{\verb~|end <index> ...~}), the same library gives its per-declaration instruction counts, comparable declaration by declaration with \code{\verb~|work/baselines/mathlib-profile.nano-lean.j1.tsv.gz~}:}

\pre{\verb~|
bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network bench/baselines/profile-mathlib.sh build selftest
bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network sh -c '
  LD_PRELOAD=$PWD/work/baselines/profile/libinstrtap.so INSTRTAP_PREFIXES="start |end " \
  work/dist/bin/rlvm-jvm --threads 1 --trace work/exports/mathlib.ndjson 2> work/rlvm/mathlib.trace'
~}

\p{Checked with a plain Java program on the project's GraalVM in JVM mode (\code{\verb~|profile-mathlib.sh selftest-java~}, \src{bench/baselines/InstrtapTest.java}): \code{\verb~|System.err.println("start …")~} goes through libc's \code{\verb~|write~}, and a loop of 1.2e9 instructions between two lines reads as 1.2007e9 and 1.2026e9 once the JIT has compiled it (0.64e9 to 0.80e9 in the three rounds before, while it runs on-stack-replaced code). The count is that of the Java thread alone: JIT compiler and GC threads are not in it, which is what one wants when asking what the checker itself spends per declaration (the difference to the whole run is then the runtime's share). Not tried with a native image; \code{\verb~|--trace~} is a suggestion, RLVM has no such switch yet. \code{\verb~|INSTRTAP_FD=1~} taps stdout instead of stderr; \code{\verb~|INSTRTAP_PREFIXES~} takes any \code{\verb~||~}-separated line prefixes.}
}

\subtree{
\title{8. Reproducing}

\pre{\verb~|
cd .

# the baselines on init, std, mathlib (about 2 h; stages can be named one by one)
bench/baselines/big-all.sh                 # = mathlib-fast-1 small mathlib-fast-23 threads mathlib-slow mathlib-official
bench/baselines/big-summary.py             # tables + bench/baselines.json

# per-declaration profiles (about 10 min) and their summary (1 min)
bench/arena-docker.sh --arena-limits --no-network bench/baselines/profile-mathlib.sh build selftest \
    nano-lean-import nano-lean-j1 gruszka-pack gruszka-w1 gruszka-check-w1 sokonanoda-parse sokonanoda-v1 \
    rpylean-jsonl nano-lean-j4 gruszka-w4 gruszka-check-w4 sokonanoda-v4
bench/baselines/profile-summary.py         # work/baselines/mathlib-profile.{json,top200.tsv,by-name.tsv.gz,*.tsv.gz}

# the generated tables of this document and of bench/README.md
bench/baselines/render-tables.py docs/research/12-baselines.md bench/README.md
~}

\p{As run on 2026-11-24: the single-worker profile steps ran in a container on CPUs 18,19 (\code{\verb~|--cpuset-cpus 18,19 --memory 16g~} instead of \code{\verb~|--arena-limits~}) while the series was using the other eight performance cores, the 4-worker steps under \code{\verb~|--arena-limits~} between two stages of the series, and the official kernel's mathlib run as \code{\verb~|OFFICIAL_CPUSET=18,19 bench/baselines/big-all.sh mathlib-official~}. Per-thread instruction counts do not depend on which performance core a thread runs on (\src{bench/README.md}, validation).}
}

\subtree{
\title{9. Caveats and what was not done}

\ul{
\li{\strong{One machine, one day.} aarch64, not the arena's x86-64; Rust 1.100.0 / 1.97.1 from rustup, not the arena's nixpkgs toolchain; PGO profiles trained here. Section 3 quantifies the difference for the baselines; for RLVM it is unknown.}
\li{\strong{Wall and CPU times were taken on a shared host} (1-minute load average 5 to 35 during the runs; other agents' containers at times on the same eight cores). They are upper bounds. Instruction counts are not affected: see the spreads.}
\li{\strong{Single runs.} nanoda, tenet-jvm-c2, tenet-jvm and official have one mathlib run each; the 1-, 2- and 8-worker rows of section 4 are one run each. The native ones repeat to 0.1 \% on init and std; a JVM run varies by 1-2 \% (5 \% was seen on init). The +28 \% of the Graal JIT over C2 on mathlib is one run against one run: far outside that noise, but not repeated and not explained.}
\li{\strong{The official kernel's mathlib run} was made on CPUs 18,19 with 16 GB instead of \code{\verb~|--arena-limits~}, next to the rest of the series. Its count (ratio to the published one 0.945; 0.949 and 0.950 on init and std) shows no effect of that.}
\li{\strong{tenet-jvm} runs on the project's GraalVM CE 25, not on the arena's OpenJDK; \code{\verb~|tenet-jvm-c2~} only switches the top-tier compiler to C2.}
\li{\strong{Profiles are from instrumented runs} (tracing switched on, the library preloaded), 1-6 \% dearer than the plain runs. gruszka's per-declaration figures are corrected by a uniform 31,600 instructions; small declarations are therefore only roughly right for gruszka (463 come out at 0). rpylean's intervals include reading and runtime work between declarations. sokonanoda has wall times only. Kinds come from sokonanoda's listing joined by name; 18,349 of rpylean's names did not match (it prints some names differently).}
\li{\strong{Not profiled:} official, nanoda, pestka, mathgraph and tenet-jvm have no per-declaration switch for exports; nothing was patched to add one.}
\li{\strong{Not measured:} the other big corpora (cslib, cedar, con-leche, navier-stokes-euler, reachability); x86-64; energy or cache behaviour; \code{\verb~|instrtap~} with a native image.}
\li{sokonanoda's bimodal count on \code{\verb~|perf/magma-list-deep-n36~} (previous infra step) did not show on the big corpora: 0.11 \% spread over 3 mathlib runs.}
\li{The "families" of expensive declarations in 5.5 are read off their names, not off their terms.}
}
}
